Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 19:20:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 585/585] arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini-station: describe the
 vibration motor

The Gemini PDA vibration motor is driven by the MT6351 VIBR LDO. The
vendor driver sets the LDO to 2.8 V once and then only switches its
enable bit, with no PWM or GPIO involved; Gemian reports the LDO at
2.8 V on this unit.

Describe the LDO held at 2.8 V and a regulator-haptic device on it, so
the motor is an ordinary force-feedback input device. Any non-zero
rumble magnitude switches the motor on at the vendor voltage.

Not yet run on hardware.
---
 .../mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts    | 22 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts
index 4711ed370..e5102fa71 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts
@@ -620,3 +620,25 @@ &mmc1 {
 	no-sdio;
 	status = "okay";
 };
+
+/*
+ * The vibration motor sits on the MT6351 VIBR LDO. The vendor driver only
+ * switches the LDO on and off and runs it at 2.8 V, so hold the LDO there
+ * and give the motor that one level.
+ */
+&mt6351regulator {
+	mt6351_vibr_reg: ldo-vibr {
+		regulator-name = "vibr";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <2800000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <2800000>;
+	};
+};
+
+/ {
+	haptic {
+		compatible = "regulator-haptic";
+		haptic-supply = <&mt6351_vibr_reg>;
+		min-microvolt = <2800000>;
+		max-microvolt = <2800000>;
+	};
+};
